  </style>

  <div class="page">
    <article class="explainer"><Hero title="Distance Correlation">
  <div class="byline">Notes on
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/0803.4101" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Szekely, Rizzo &amp; Bakirov (2007), <em>Measuring and Testing Dependence by Correlation of Distances</em></a>
          and
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.2926" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Szekely &amp; Rizzo (2014), <em>Partial Distance Correlation with Methods for Dissimilarities</em></a>.</div>
</Hero>

        <p class="lead">
          The 2007 paper introduced <strong>distance correlation</strong>, a scalar coefficient that is zero exactly when two random vectors are independent. Unlike Pearson&rsquo;s $r$, it is not limited to linear dependence. The construction starts with pairwise distance matrices, subtracts row and column means to remove uninformative structure, and averages the entry-wise product.
        </p>
        <p class="lead">
          The 2014 paper replaces the 2007 double-centering with <em>U-centering</em>. With that centering, the same bilinear form becomes a positive semidefinite <strong>inner product</strong> on a Hilbert space of centered matrices. That gives an unbiased estimator, lengths and angles, orthogonal projection for <em>partial</em> distance correlation, and a version that works for non-Euclidean dissimilarities.
        </p>

        <div class="part-divider">Part I &middot; <span class="part-name">Distance correlation (2007)</span></div>

        <h2 id="pearson">1. What Pearson&rsquo;s r misses</h2>
        <p>
          Pearson&rsquo;s $r$ is a coefficient of <em>linear</em> dependence: it measures whether the cloud of points stretches along a diagonal. Symmetric nonlinear relationships, such as a parabola, a sine wave, or a circle, can have $r &bsol;approx 0$ while $X$ and $Y$ are tightly coupled, even deterministic. Rank-based generalizations (Spearman, Kendall) fix monotone misses but not the symmetric ones. The 2007 paper&rsquo;s motivating quote: &ldquo;distance correlation is zero only if the random vectors are independent.&rdquo;
        </p>

        <div class="panel">
          <div class="controls">
            <label>Relationship
              <select id="d1-shape">
                <option value="linear">Linear</option>
                <option value="quadratic" selected>Quadratic</option>
                <option value="sine">Sine wave</option>
                <option value="circle">Circle</option>
                <option value="cross">Cross</option>
                <option value="independent">Independent noise</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <label>Noise
              <input type="range" id="d1-noise" min="0" max="100" value="15" />
              <span id="d1-noise-val" class="mono" style="width:32px;display:inline-block">0.15</span>
            </label>
            <button id="d1-resample">Resample</button>
          </div>
          <svg id="d1-scatter" viewBox="0 0 360 220" width="360" height="220" style="margin: 0 auto;"></svg>
          <div class="readout">
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">Pearson r</div><div class="val" id="d1-pearson">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">Spearman $&bsol;rho$</div><div class="val" id="d1-spearman">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">Distance correlation</div><div class="val" id="d1-dcor">&mdash;</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="legend">
            Spearman&rsquo;s $&bsol;rho$ patches the linearity assumption for <em>monotone</em> relationships, but still collapses on the symmetric ones (parabola, circle, cross). The 2007 paper specifically calls out rank-based tests as &ldquo;ineffective for testing nonmonotone types of dependence,&rdquo; precisely the gap distance correlation closes.
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="recipe">2. The 2007 definition</h2>
        <p>
          The population coefficient. Take a weighted $L^2$ distance between the joint characteristic function $&bsol;phi_&lbrace;X,Y&rbrace;$ and the product of marginals $&bsol;phi_X&bsol;phi_Y$:
        </p>
        <div class="formula">
          $$V^2(X,Y) = &bsol;int |&bsol;phi_&lbrace;X,Y&rbrace;(t,s) - &bsol;phi_X(t)&bsol;phi_Y(s)|^2 w(t,s)&bsol;,dt&bsol;,ds$$
        </div>
        <p>
          The weight $w(t,s) &bsol;propto |t|^&lbrace;-(1+p)&rbrace; |s|^&lbrace;-(1+q)&rbrace;$ is non-integrable on purpose: integrable weights collapse this measure to $&bsol;rho^2$ in the small-signal limit and so cannot distinguish dependence from independence. Under finite first moments, the integral reduces to a compact expected-distance identity:
        </p>
        <div class="formula">
          $$V^2(X,Y) = &bsol;mathbb&lbrace;E&rbrace;|X-X'||Y-Y'| + &bsol;mathbb&lbrace;E&rbrace;|X-X'|&bsol;,&bsol;mathbb&lbrace;E&rbrace;|Y-Y'| - 2&bsol;mathbb&lbrace;E&rbrace;|X-X'||Y-Y''|$$
        </div>
        <p>
          which is what the sample statistic estimates. Form the matrix of pairwise distances $a_&lbrace;ij&rbrace; = |X_i - X_j|$, <em>double-center</em> it (subtract row means, column means, add back the grand mean), do the same for $Y$, and take the entry-wise mean of the product:
        </p>
        <div class="formula">
          $$V_n^2(X,Y) = &lbrace;1&bsol;over n^2&rbrace;&bsol;sum_&lbrace;i,j&rbrace; A_&lbrace;ij&rbrace;B_&lbrace;ij&rbrace;, &bsol;qquad dCor_n = &lbrace;V_n(X,Y)&bsol;over &bsol;sqrt&lbrace;V_n(X)V_n(Y)&rbrace;&rbrace;$$
        </div>
        <p>
          Under finite first moments, $V^2(X,Y) = 0$ iff $X$ and $Y$ are independent, in arbitrary, possibly different, Euclidean dimensions. This is the 2007 paper&rsquo;s headline theorem; everything else follows from it.
        </p>
        <p>
          For one classical reference point: when $(X,Y)$ are bivariate standard normal with correlation $&bsol;rho$, distance correlation has a closed form (Theorem 7). It always lies <em>below</em> $|&bsol;rho|$, with the ratio $R(X,Y)/|&bsol;rho|$ bottoming out near $0.891$ as $&bsol;rho &bsol;to 0$:
        </p>

        <div class="panel">
          <div class="controls">
            <label>$&bsol;rho$ (population correlation)
              <input type="range" id="dbvn-rho" min="-95" max="95" value="50" />
              <span id="dbvn-rho-val" class="mono" style="width:42px;display:inline-block">+0.50</span>
            </label>
            <button id="dbvn-resample">Resample</button>
          </div>
          <div class="row">
            <div>
              <svg id="dbvn-scatter" viewBox="0 0 240 240" width="240" height="240"></svg>
              <div class="caption">Bivariate normal sample (n = 400)</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <svg id="dbvn-curve" viewBox="0 0 320 240" width="320" height="240"></svg>
              <div class="caption">$R^2(&bsol;rho)$ and $&bsol;rho^2$: closed form (solid) vs. empirical $dCor^2$ (dot)</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="readout">
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">Pearson r (sample)</div><div class="val dim" id="dbvn-r">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">R (closed form)</div><div class="val" id="dbvn-R">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">dCor (sample)</div><div class="val" id="dbvn-dcor">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">$R / |&bsol;rho|$</div><div class="val dim" id="dbvn-ratio">&mdash;</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="legend">
            Even in the one case where Pearson is the gold standard, distance correlation is a smooth, slightly attenuated version of $|&bsol;rho|$. The interest of dCor isn&rsquo;t this regime; it&rsquo;s everywhere else, where $&bsol;rho$ fails entirely.
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="test">3. Testing independence</h2>
        <p>
          The sample statistic $V_n^2$ is non-negative but has no clean closed-form null distribution. The 2007 paper proved that under independence and finite first moments, $nV_n^2$ converges in distribution to a quadratic form $&bsol;sum_j &bsol;lambda_j Z_j^2$ in i.i.d. standard normals, with eigenvalues that depend on the distribution of $(X,Y)$. Useful asymptotically, but distribution-dependent. The practical recommendation is a <strong>permutation test</strong>: hold $X$ fixed, shuffle the $Y$ values to break any real dependence, recompute the statistic, and see how often the shuffled value exceeds the observed one.
        </p>

        <div class="panel">
          <div class="controls">
            <label>Shape
              <select id="dperm-shape">
                <option value="quadratic" selected>Quadratic</option>
                <option value="sine">Sine</option>
                <option value="circle">Circle</option>
                <option value="independent">Independent</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <label>Noise
              <input type="range" id="dperm-noise" min="0" max="100" value="20" />
              <span id="dperm-noise-val" class="mono" style="width:32px;display:inline-block">0.20</span>
            </label>
            <button id="dperm-run">Run 500 permutations</button>
          </div>
          <div class="row">
            <div>
              <svg id="dperm-scatter" viewBox="0 0 240 240" width="240" height="240"></svg>
              <div class="caption">Sample (n = 60)</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <svg id="dperm-hist" viewBox="0 0 360 240" width="360" height="240"></svg>
              <div class="caption">Permutation null for $V_n^2$; observed in red</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="readout">
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">Observed $V_n^2$</div><div class="val" id="dperm-obs">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">Permutation p-value</div><div class="val" id="dperm-p">&mdash;</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="legend">
            Power comparisons in the 2007 paper (Examples 1&ndash;3) show the dCov-based test matching Wilks&rsquo; LRT on linear Gaussian alternatives and clearly dominating it, along with rank-based tests, on multiplicative-noise and log-quadratic alternatives.
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>What it replaced</h3>
        <p>
          Before 2007, each standard way to test for dependence between two random vectors had its own narrow regime:
        </p>
        <ul style="font-size:14px;margin:0 0 12px 20px;">
          <li><strong>Pearson&rsquo;s $r$ and Wilks&rsquo; likelihood-ratio test.</strong> Optimal under Gaussianity. Heavy tails or nonlinear coupling break them; the 2007 paper shows Wilks&rsquo; LRT with inflated Type-I error on $t_1$-distributed data, and near-zero power on multiplicative-noise alternatives.</li>
          <li><strong>Spearman&rsquo;s $&bsol;rho$, Kendall&rsquo;s $&bsol;tau$, Puri-Sen rank correlation.</strong> Distribution-free for <em>monotone</em> alternatives. Power flatlines on symmetric non-monotone dependence (parabolas, sinusoids, multiplicative noise), visible directly in &sect; 1 above.</li>
          <li><strong>The Mantel test (1967).</strong> Permutation correlation between two raw distance matrices. Widely used in ecology, but its statistic does not double-center the matrices, so it is not a consistent test of independence; it can return zero when $X$ and $Y$ are dependent. The 2014 paper compares its partial-dCor test directly against partial Mantel and dominates it.</li>
          <li><strong>Hoeffding&rsquo;s $D$, Blum-Kiefer-Rosenblatt.</strong> Genuine if-and-only-if independence coefficients via the joint vs. product CDFs, but defined only for <em>bivariate continuous</em> distributions. They do not extend to vectors in $&bsol;mathbb&lbrace;R&rbrace;^p &bsol;times &bsol;mathbb&lbrace;R&rbrace;^q$.</li>
          <li><strong>Mutual information estimators (Kraskov et al. 2004, kernel-based).</strong> Also characterize independence in any dimension, but require bandwidth or k-neighbour tuning and don&rsquo;t produce a single canonical scalar.</li>
          <li><strong>HSIC (Gretton et al. 2005).</strong> A near-contemporaneous kernel-based independence criterion, conceptually very close: also an inner product of centered objects in a Hilbert space (an RKHS). The two were later shown to coincide for a particular distance-induced kernel (Sejdinovic et al. 2013).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          What distance correlation offers is a single scalar in $[0,1]$ that is parameter-free, defined in arbitrary dimensions, characterizes independence exactly, has a tractable permutation test, and is competitive with the LRT in the Gaussian regime while dominating it elsewhere.
        </p>

        <div class="part-divider">Part II &middot; <span class="part-name">Partial dCor and dissimilarities (2014)</span></div>

        <div class="framing">
          The 2007 sample statistic $V_n^2 = n^&lbrace;-2&rbrace;&bsol;sum A_&lbrace;ij&rbrace;B_&lbrace;ij&rbrace;$ is a <em>bilinear form</em> on double-centered matrices, but it is not a clean inner product on a Hilbert space; it is biased, and there is no natural projection geometry on which to build a partial-correlation analogue. The 2014 paper&rsquo;s observation is that a slightly different centering rule resolves all of these issues. Replace double-centering with <em>U-centering</em> ($&bsol;widetilde A$), and the bilinear form
          $(&bsol;widetilde A &bsol;cdot &bsol;widetilde B) = &lbrace;1&bsol;over n(n-3)&rbrace;&bsol;sum_&lbrace;i&bsol;ne j&rbrace;&bsol;widetilde A_&lbrace;ij&rbrace;&bsol;widetilde B_&lbrace;ij&rbrace;$
          becomes a positive semidefinite inner product on a Hilbert space $H_n$ of centered matrices. The general recipe: pick a centering that removes the nuisance row, column, and grand-mean structure so the residuals&rsquo; bilinear form (a) still captures dependence and (b) is PSD. Three things follow:
          <ul>
            <li>The inner product is an <strong>unbiased</strong> estimator of $V^2(X,Y)$ (&sect; 4 below).</li>
            <li>Length, angle, orthogonality, and <strong>orthogonal projection</strong> become legitimate operations, and partial distance correlation is the cosine of the angle between residuals after projecting out a third matrix (&sect; 5).</li>
            <li>The inner product is <strong>invariant to additive shifts</strong> of the underlying dissimilarities, so any symmetric zero-diagonal dissimilarity matrix, not just Euclidean distances, plugs straight in (&sect; 6).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="unbiased">4. The unbiased estimator</h2>
        <p>
          Where the double-centered estimator $V_n^2$ divides row and column sums by $n$ and the grand sum by $n^2$, U-centering uses $n-2$ and $(n-1)(n-2)$, the &ldquo;leave-one-out&rdquo; counts that make the expectation algebra come out cleanly:
        </p>
        <div class="formula">
          $$&bsol;widetilde A_&lbrace;ij&rbrace; = a_&lbrace;ij&rbrace; - &lbrace;a_&lbrace;i&bsol;cdot&rbrace;&bsol;over n-2&rbrace; - &lbrace;a_&lbrace;&bsol;cdot j&rbrace;&bsol;over n-2&rbrace; + &lbrace;a_&lbrace;&bsol;cdot&bsol;cdot&rbrace;&bsol;over (n-1)(n-2)&rbrace;, &bsol;qquad i&bsol;ne j$$
        </div>
        <p>
          Diagonal entries are set to zero. Proposition 1: $(&bsol;widetilde A &bsol;cdot &bsol;widetilde B)$ is an <em>unbiased</em> estimator of the population $V^2(X,Y)$. Below: a Monte Carlo comparing the 2007 estimator with its 2014 replacement as $n$ increases.
        </p>

        <div class="panel">
          <div class="controls">
            <label>True relationship
              <select id="d2-scenario">
                <option value="indep" selected>X ⟂ Y   (true V² = 0)</option>
                <option value="dep">Y = X² + noise   (true V² > 0)</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <label>Replicates per n
              <select id="d2-reps">
                <option value="50">50</option>
                <option value="200" selected>200</option>
                <option value="800">800</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <button id="d2-run">Run simulation</button>
            <span id="d2-progress" class="mono" style="color:var(--text-dim)"></span>
          </div>
          <svg id="d2-plot" viewBox="0 0 540 280" width="540" height="280" style="margin: 0 auto;"></svg>
          <div class="legend" style="text-align:center; margin-top: 4px;">
            <span class="key" style="background: var(--neg)"></span> biased $V_n^2$ (double-centered) &nbsp;
            <span class="key" style="background: var(--accent)"></span> unbiased $(&bsol;widetilde A&bsol;cdot&bsol;widetilde B)$ (U-centered) &nbsp;
            <span class="key" style="background: var(--pos)"></span> long-run truth
          </div>
          <p class="footnote" style="margin-top:8px">
            With $X &bsol;perp Y$ the truth is exactly zero, and the bias of $V_n^2$ is most visible: positive, decaying like $1/n$. The unbiased estimator fluctuates symmetrically around zero (it can be negative; remember, it is an inner product, not a squared length).
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="geometry">5. The Hilbert-space picture</h2>
        <p>
          The U-centered matrices live in a Hilbert space $H_n$ with the inner product above. Once we have an inner product, every concept from Euclidean geometry (lengths, angles, orthogonal projections) transfers automatically. <em>Partial distance correlation</em> is just the standard geometric construction:
        </p>
        <ol style="font-size:14px;margin:0 0 12px 20px;">
          <li>Form U-centered matrices $&bsol;widetilde A, &bsol;widetilde B, &bsol;widetilde C$ from $X, Y, Z$.</li>
          <li>Project $&bsol;widetilde A$ and $&bsol;widetilde B$ onto the orthogonal complement of $&bsol;widetilde C$. Call the residuals $P_&lbrace;Z^&bsol;perp&rbrace;(&bsol;widetilde A) = &bsol;widetilde A - &bsol;alpha&bsol;widetilde C$ and $P_&lbrace;Z^&bsol;perp&rbrace;(&bsol;widetilde B) = &bsol;widetilde B - &bsol;beta&bsol;widetilde C$.</li>
          <li>$R^*(X,Y;Z)$ is the cosine of the angle between the residuals.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The picture below is drawn from <em>actual</em> U-centered inner products: vector lengths and pairwise angles all reflect what the math says. Slide the controls to reshape the data and watch the residual angle, the partial distance correlation, change.
        </p>

        <div class="panel">
          <div class="controls">
            <label>Z &rarr; X strength
              <input type="range" id="d3-sx" min="0" max="100" value="70" />
              <span id="d3-sx-val" class="mono" style="width:32px;display:inline-block">0.70</span>
            </label>
            <label>Z &rarr; Y strength
              <input type="range" id="d3-sy" min="0" max="100" value="70" />
              <span id="d3-sy-val" class="mono" style="width:32px;display:inline-block">0.70</span>
            </label>
            <label>Direct X &rarr; Y
              <input type="range" id="d3-dxy" min="-100" max="100" value="0" />
              <span id="d3-dxy-val" class="mono" style="width:42px;display:inline-block">+0.00</span>
            </label>
            <button id="d3-resample">Resample</button>
          </div>
          <svg id="d3-geom" viewBox="0 0 540 320" width="540" height="320" style="margin: 0 auto;"></svg>
          <div class="caption">Vectors live in $H_n$; they are sketched here in 2D using their true pairwise angles (the angle between $&bsol;widetilde A$ and $&bsol;widetilde B$ is exact; the placement of $&bsol;widetilde A$ and $&bsol;widetilde B$ on either side of $&bsol;widetilde C$ is chosen so the partial cosine is realised.)</div>
          <div class="readout">
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">R*(X,Y)</div><div class="val" id="d3-rxy">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">R*(X,Z)</div><div class="val dim" id="d3-rxz">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">R*(Y,Z)</div><div class="val dim" id="d3-ryz">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">R*(X,Y;Z)</div><div class="val" id="d3-rxyz">&mdash;</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="legend">
            <span class="key" style="background: var(--x-color)"></span> $&bsol;widetilde A$ (from X) &nbsp;
            <span class="key" style="background: var(--y-color)"></span> $&bsol;widetilde B$ (from Y) &nbsp;
            <span class="key" style="background: var(--z-color)"></span> $&bsol;widetilde C$ (from Z). Dashed arrows are residuals after projecting out $&bsol;widetilde C$.
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          With direct link at zero, $X$ and $Y$ share only their $Z$ pathway: $&bsol;widetilde A$ and $&bsol;widetilde B$ both lean toward $&bsol;widetilde C$, and once that shared component is subtracted, the residuals are nearly perpendicular, with $R^*(X,Y;Z) &bsol;approx 0$. Move the direct slider, and the residuals swing back into alignment. The paper notes that $R^*(X,Y;Z) = 0$ does <em>not</em> in general imply conditional independence; it characterises orthogonality in $H_n$, which is strictly weaker.
        </p>

        <div class="formula">
          $$R^*(X,Y;Z) = &lbrace;R^*(X,Y) - R^*(X,Z)R^*(Y,Z)&bsol;over &bsol;sqrt&lbrace;(1 - R^*(X,Z)^2)(1 - R^*(Y,Z)^2)&rbrace;&rbrace;$$
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          The familiar partial-correlation formula falls out as Proposition 2 of the paper, a direct consequence of the projection geometry. Note that here $R^*$ plays the role that <em>squared</em> dCor plays elsewhere, so its values are on the scale of $dCor^2$ and can be negative.
        </p>

        <h2 id="dissim">6. Beyond Euclidean distances</h2>
        <p>
          In ecology, genetics, and psychometrics, &ldquo;dissimilarities&rdquo; often violate the triangle inequality; Bray-Curtis on species counts is a standard example. The paper&rsquo;s second contribution: distance-correlation methods still work, because U-centering only sees the <em>inner product</em>, not the original dissimilarities.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two facts make this go. <strong>Theorem 2</strong>: every element of $H_n$ is the U-centered distance matrix of some Euclidean point configuration in $&bsol;mathbb&lbrace;R&rbrace;^p$ ($p &bsol;le n - 2$), recoverable via classical multidimensional scaling. <strong>Lemma 1(iv)</strong>: U-centering is <em>invariant</em> to adding a constant $c$ to every off-diagonal dissimilarity (a &ldquo;Cailliez constant&rdquo; commonly used to force Euclidean embedding). The recovered MDS configuration moves around as $c$ changes, but the inner product, and hence every dCor statistic, does not.
        </p>

        <div class="panel">
          <div class="controls">
            <button id="d4-resample">New dataset</button>
            <label>Cailliez constant c
              <input type="range" id="d4-c" min="0" max="200" value="0" />
              <span id="d4-c-val" class="mono" style="width:42px;display:inline-block">+0.00</span>
            </label>
          </div>
          <div class="row">
            <div>
              <svg id="d4-mat-bc" viewBox="0 0 220 220" width="220" height="220"></svg>
              <div class="caption">Bray-Curtis dissimilarities ($D + c$)</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <svg id="d4-mat-uc" viewBox="0 0 220 220" width="220" height="220"></svg>
              <div class="caption">U-centered ($&bsol;widetilde A$): identical for every $c$</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <svg id="d4-mds" viewBox="0 0 220 220" width="220" height="220"></svg>
              <div class="caption">Classical MDS recovery (2D)</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="readout">
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">Min MDS eigenvalue</div><div class="val dim" id="d4-tri">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">$|&bsol;widetilde A|^2$ (invariant)</div><div class="val" id="d4-norm">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">R*(species, env)</div><div class="val" id="d4-rxy">&mdash;</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="legend">
            Eight sites with simulated species counts; the second matrix is built from a related environmental gradient. The Bray-Curtis matrix is a metric but not generally Euclidean, so classical MDS produces a negative eigenvalue at $c = 0$ (Euclidean embedding is impossible). Sliding $c$ upward (the Cailliez additive constant) pushes that eigenvalue toward zero and rearranges the embedding, but the U-centered matrix and the R* readout are unchanged, as Lemma 1(iv) guarantees.
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="network-recovery">7. Network recovery: Pearson, partial, and distance correlation</h2>
        <p>
          Bayesian-network skeleton recovery from observational data is a classic use of
          correlation measures. Here data come from a known 4-node DAG: $A\to B$, $A\to C$,
          $B\to D$, $C\to D$. Marginally $A$ and $D$ are correlated through their two paths
          even though there is no direct edge between them. Pairwise Pearson correlation
          puts an edge wherever any two variables co-vary, so it cannot tell the indirect
          $A\!-\!D$ path from the direct ones.
          <a href="/math/bayesian-graphical-models/#partial-correlation">Partial correlation</a>
          conditions on the other variables (the precision matrix, the inverse covariance)
          and recovers the direct-edge structure. Distance correlation has stronger power against non-linear dependence
          but, like raw Pearson, does not condition out indirect paths.
        </p>

        <div class="panel">
          <div class="controls">
            <button id="d7-resample">New dataset</button>
            <label>sample size N
              <input type="range" id="d7-n" min="40" max="500" step="20" value="200" />
              <span id="d7-n-val" class="mono" style="width:42px;display:inline-block">200</span>
            </label>
            <label>threshold τ
              <input type="range" id="d7-tau" min="0.03" max="0.6" step="0.01" value="0.16" />
              <span id="d7-tau-val" class="mono" style="width:42px;display:inline-block">0.16</span>
            </label>
          </div>
          <div class="row">
            <div>
              <svg id="d7-true" viewBox="0 0 180 180" width="180" height="180"></svg>
              <div class="caption">true skeleton</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <svg id="d7-pearson" viewBox="0 0 180 180" width="180" height="180"></svg>
              <div class="caption">Pearson |r| ≥ τ</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <svg id="d7-partial" viewBox="0 0 180 180" width="180" height="180"></svg>
              <div class="caption">partial correlation |ρ| ≥ τ</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <svg id="d7-dcor" viewBox="0 0 180 180" width="180" height="180"></svg>
              <div class="caption">dCor ≥ τ</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="readout">
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">Pearson</div><div class="val" id="d7-pearson-stat">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">Partial</div><div class="val" id="d7-partial-stat">&mdash;</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="label">dCor</div><div class="val" id="d7-dcor-stat">&mdash;</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="legend">
            The true skeleton has 4 edges (A–B, A–C, B–D, C–D). The spurious A–D appears in raw
            Pearson and dCor because $A$ and $D$ share two indirect paths. Partial correlation
            conditions on $B$ and $C$ and removes it. Stats show recovered edges as TP/FP out of
            the 6 candidate pairs at the current threshold. <strong>Click any edge</strong> (true,
            recovered, or omitted) to see its Pearson / partial / dCor numeric values.
          </div>
        </div>

